===Etymology 1===
{{etymon|en|id=Q193472|:inh|enm:logge<id:log>}}
{{wp}}
[[File:Log pile in Old Park Bottom Wood - geograph.org.uk - 1932189.jpg|thumb|A '''log''' pile ({{senseno|en|Q193472}})]]

From {{inh|en|enm|logge}}, {{m|enm|logg}} (first recorded in Anglo-Latin as {{m|la|loggum}}), of {{unc|en|nocap=1}} origin,<ref>T. F. Hoad. "[https://www.encyclopedia.com/literature-and-arts/art-and-architecture/architecture/log log]." ''The Concise Oxford Dictionary of English Etymology''</ref> but probably from {{der|en|non|lóg}}, {{m|non|lág|t=felled tree, log}}, derived from {{der|en|non|liggja|t=to lie}}. If so, then cognate with {{cog|no|låg|t=fallen tree}}.

Alternatively, directly from {{bor|en|no|låg|t=fallen tree}}, which could have been borrowed through the Norwegian timber trade.<ref>{{R:CDOE}}, p. 607.</ref> However the Old Norse/Middle Norwegian vowel is long while Middle English vowel is short.<ref>https://blog.oup.com/2018/06/etymology-gleanings-may-2018-part-2/</ref>

